## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical understanding of time-based caching and deterministic testing, emphasizing a beginner-friendly approach to isolate time and external dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- TTL semantics for in-memory cache
- Distinguishing cache miss vs hit
- Time mocking (fake timers) in tests
- Avoiding API calls on cache hits

## Code Example

```javascript
class Cache {
  constructor(ttlMs) { this.ttl = ttlMs; this.map = new Map(); }
  async get(key, fetcher) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const rec = this.map.get(key);
    if (rec && now - rec.ts < this.ttl) return rec.value;
    const value = await fetcher(key);
    this.map.set(key, { value, ts: now });
    return value;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you mock time in Jest/Mocha?
- How would you test behavior under API latency or errors?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first behavior are core for reliability but beginners often struggle with storage APIs and network conditions. This question isolates those concepts in a tangible, front-end context.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first strategy
- IndexedDB usage
- Sync mechanics and idempotence
- Conflict resolution policy
- CI network condition emulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for offline caching test
describe('Offline-first sync', () => {
  it('caches submissions offline and syncs when online', () => {
    cy.visit('/form');
    cy.window().then(w => { w.navigator.onLine = false; });
    cy.get('#name').type('Alice');
    cy.get('#email').type('a@example.com');
    cy.get('#submit').click();
    // verify IndexedDB contains 1 entry
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests for different storage backends (IndexedDB vs LocalStorage)?
- How would you measure flakiness and improve CI stability?

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end testing skills focusing on input validation, idempotency, queueing, rate limiting, and privacy in logs; uses realistic dataflow rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing with real components vs mocks
- Idempotency keys for duplicate requests
- Rate-limiting and concurrency testing
- Log scrubbing and PII protection
- CI reproducibility with fixtures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest + supertest skeleton for /feedback endpoint
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../src/app');

test('idempotent submission', async () => {
  const payload = { user: 'alice', feedback: 'great', idempotencyKey: 'abc-123' };
  await request(app).post('/feedback').send(payload).expect(201);
  // second attempt with same key should be treated as duplicate
  await request(app).post('/feedback').send(payload).expect(409);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests for non-deterministic services (e.g., occasional DB hiccups)?
- What strategies would you use to measure and reduce test flakiness in CI?

